                             break and count\\n    # For x0 in A: we            
                             can do it.\\n    # For x0 in B: we                 
                             compute f(x0)=b0 (which is <=800), then            
                             need steps_to_one[b0] <= k-1\\n\\n    #            
                             So algorithm:\\n    # Precompute                   
                             steps_to_one for y in 1..800 by BFS                
                             from 1\\n    # Then for all x0 in                  
                             [1..min(m,800)]: simulate up to k                  
                             steps\\n    # For x0 >800 and <=m:                 
                             compute b0 = popcount(x0), then if                 
                             steps_to_one[b0] <= k-1 then count                 
                             x0\\n\\n    # But how to count how many            
                             x0 in (800, m] have popcount(x0)=b0 and            
                             steps_to_one[b0] <=k-1 ?\\n    # Let               
                             C(b) = count of numbers in (800, m]                
                             with popcount = b\\n    # Then total               
                             for B is sum_{b: steps_to_one[b] <=k-1}            
                             C(b)\\n\\n    # To compute C(b), note              
                             that it\'s equal to (count_up_to(m, b)             
                             - count_up_to(800, b))\\n    # So we               
                             need count_up_to(X, b) for X=m and                 
                             X=800\\n\\n    # Implement count_up_to             
                             as the digit DP above\\n\\n    # Now               
                             the plan is:\\n    # 1. Precompute                 
                             steps_to_one for y in 1..800 using BFS             
                             from 1\\n    # 2. For A, for x0 in                 
                             1..min(m,800), simulate up to k steps,             
