                             # The total subsets of elements                    
                             divisible by g is 2^{cnt[g]} - 1                   
                             (nonempty)\\n        # But we want                 
                             those subsets whose reduced set has gcd            
                             1: so ans * 2^{(total elements                     
                             divisible by g) - (number in m)}\\n                
                             # Wait actually m is exactly the                   
                             elements divisible by g, so the number             
                             of subsets of m is 2^{n}-1\\n        #             
                             But ans is already the number of                   
                             nonempty subsets of m with gcd 1 (since            
                             we did non-empty)\\n        # So the               
                             number of subsets of nums (original)               
                             with gcd exactly g is ans * (number of             
                             ways to choose the rest? No, since all             
                             elements must be divisible by g)\\n                
                             # Actually, every subset counted in ans            
                             is a subset of the elements divisible              
                             by g. So the total number is ans.\\n               
                             # But no: the subsets of m (elements               
                             divisible by g) are exactly the subsets            
                             of the original nums that are composed             
                             of elements divisible by g. To get                 
                             subsets whose gcd is exactly g (not a              
                             multiple), we need those subsets of m              
                             that have gcd 1 when divided by g (so              
                             original gcd is g).\\n        # Thus               
                             the count F[g] = ans\\n                            
                             subset_counts[g] = ans\\n    return                
                             subset_counts\\n\\ndef                             
                             count_subsequence_pairs(nums:                      
                             List[int]) -> int:\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n              
                             Finds the number of pairs of non-empty             
                             disjoint subsequences (seq1, seq2) such            
                             that\\n    gcd of seq1 = gcd of seq2,              
                             modulo 1e9+7.\\n    \\n    Args:\\n                
                             nums: List of integers\\n    \\n                   
                             Returns:\\n        The required count              
                             modulo 1e9+7\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    #               
                             Precompute all subset counts for each              
                             possible gcd value\\n    gcd_to_subsets            
                             = compute_subset_gcd_counts(nums)\\n               
                             N = len(nums)\\n    # Precompute powers            
                             of 2 up to N for combinations\\n                   
                             pow2 = [1]*(N+1)\\n    for i in                    
                             range(1, N+1):\\n        pow2[i] =                 
                             (pow2[i-1]*2) % MOD\\n\\n    from                  
                             collections import defaultdict\\n                  
                             total_pairs = 0\\n    # For each                   
                             possible GCD value g, count the number             
                             of subsets with this gcd: S_g\\n    #              
                             Any pair of disjoint subsets (A,B)                 
                             where A has gcd g and B has gcd g                  
                             (could be same elements but disjoint               
                             via indices)\\n    # The total number              
                             of ways to pick two disjoint non-empty             
                             subsets both with gcd=g is:\\n    #                
                             (2^{c} - 2 - c) * (2^{c} - 2 - c) / 2 ?            
                             No.\\n    # Actually, each element can             
                             be in A, in B, in neither. But A and B             
                             must be nonempty and disjoint.\\n    #             
                             Let c = number of elements divisible by            
                             g (i.e., elements where x % g ==0)\\n              
                             # The number of ways to choose a                   
                             non-empty subset A of these (with                  
                             gcd=g) is F_g, and similarly for B, but            
                             must be disjoint.\\n    # So for each              
                             element divisible by g, we have 3                  
                             choices: in A, in B, or in neither, but            
                             A and B cannot be empty.\\n    # So                
                             total ways: (3^c - 2*(2^c -1)) /2 ? Not            
                             exactly, inclusion-exclusion                       
                             required.\\n    # Alternatively, think             
                             as first choosing any pair of disjoint             
                             subsets (A,B), both non-empty, from the            
                             elements divisible by g.\\n    # The               
                             number of such ordered pairs is: (2^{c}            
                             - 1)^2 - (2^{c-1})^2  [subtract when A             
                             and B overlap]\\n    # But actually,               
                             the correct count is: For each element,            
                             there are 3 choices (A, B, neither),               
                             subtract cases where A is empty or B is            
                             empty.\\n    # So total = (3^c - 2*(2^c            
                             -1))\\n    # But we need unordered                 
                             pairs of subsets (since (A,B) same as              
                             (B,A)), so divide by 2.\\n    # But                
                             actually in the problem, the pairs                 
                             (seq1,seq2) are ordered (because the               
                             doc says pairs (seq1,seq2) but usually             
                             considered ordered unless specified                
                             otherwise). The problem\'s sample input            
                             [1,2,3,4] -> 10, which matches 15 total            
                             nonempty subsets choose 2 ordered                  
                             disjoint but actually the standard                 
                             interpretation is unordered pairs of               
                             subset indices with gcd equality.\\n               
                             # However, known solution approach: for            
                             each g, let c be the count of elements             
                             divisible by g. The number of ways to              
                             pick two disjoint nonempty subsets with            
                             gcd=g is (2^{c} - 2)^2 /2 ? No.\\n    #            
                             The standard solution is:\\n    # For a            
                             given g, let cnt_g be the number of                
                             elements divisible by g.\\n    # The               
                             number of non-empty subsets with gcd ==            
                             g is S_g (from above).\\n    # The                 
                             number of ways to pick two disjoint                
                             subsets (order doesn\'t matter) is:\\n             
                             # (2^{cnt_g} - 2) * (2^{cnt_g} - 2) / 2            
                             - but this counts all pairs of nonempty            
                             subsets, not necessarily with gcd=g.\\n            
                             # Actually correct approach:\\n    #               
                             For elements divisible by g (call this             
                             set E of size m), the number of ways to            
                             choose two disjoint non-empty subsets              
                             A,B (order matters?) such that gcd                 
                             conditions hold is:\\n    # For ordered            
                             pairs: sum over all A,B disjoint,                  
                             nonempty, both with gcd=g of 1.\\n    #            
                             But since we already computed S_g as               
                             the number of nonempty subsets of E                
                             with the reduced gcd=1 (i.e. original              
                             gcd=g), the number of ordered pairs                
                             (A,B) where A and B are any two                    
                             (possibly same index but must be                   
                             disjoint via element indices) nonempty             
                             subsets of the elements divisible by g             
                             is:\\n    # (S_g * (2^{m - |A|} -1 ))              
                             summed over all subsets A. This is                 
                             complex.\\n    # The known formula is:             
                             For the elements divisible by g (m                 
                             elements), the total number of ordered             
                             pairs of disjoint nonempty subsets is              
                             (3^m - 2*2^m +1). Because each element             
                             can be in A, in B, or in neither (3^m),            
                             subtract cases where A is empty (2^m),             
                             B empty (2^m), add back the case where             
                             both are empty (1).\\n    # Then among             
                             these, the number of pairs where both              
                             have gcd exactly g (when considered in             
                             the reduced set) is (S_g)^2 * (number              
                             of ways to choose A and B from the m               
                             elements such that they are disjoint               
                             and both are in the S_g count). But                
                             since S_g counts all nonempty subsets              
                             of E (the elements divisible by g) with            
                             the required property, any A and B                 
                             chosen from E (disjoint) will have the             
                             same property if their reduced gcd is              
                             1.\\n    # Actually, the reduced set               
                             for any subset of E (elements /g) will             
                             have gcd 1 iff the original subset\'s              
                             gcd was g.\\n    # Thus the number of              
                             ordered pairs (A,B) of disjoint                    
                             nonempty subsets among the m elements,             
                             where both have gcd=g (original), is               
                             equal to the number of ordered pairs of            
                             nonempty subsets of E that are                     
                             disjoint: (3^m - 2*2^m +1)\\n    # But             
                             only those pairs where both subsets are            
                             counted in S_g. But since S_g is the               
                             number of nonempty subsets of E with               
                             the property, and any subset of E when             
                             divided by g will have gcd 1 (since we             
                             already used inclusion-exclusion), thus            
                             EVERY nonempty subset of E is in                   
                             S_g.\\n    # Therefore the number is               
                             simply the number of ordered pairs of              
                             nonempty, disjoint subsets of the m                
                             elements: (3^m - 2*2^m +1)\\n    # And             
                             since the problem asks for pairs                   
                             (seq1,seq2) where order matters? The               
                             sample input [1,2,3,4] gives 10.\\n                
                             # Let\'s see: m=4. 3^4=81, 2^4=16. So              
                             81 - 2*16 +1 = 81-32+1=50. Then 50                 
                             includes all ordered pairs of nonempty             
                             disjoint subsets. But the answer is                
