                             from the sample computations, it seems             
                             that the intended answer is sum over v             
                             of (c_v choose 2) * 2^{n -                         
                             average_size_of subsets_with_gcd_v} ...            
                             but not sure.\\n        #                          
                             Alternatively, the number of ways to               
                             pick two disjoint subsets (A,B) both               
                             with gcd v is (sum_{A subset with gcd              
                             v} 2^{n - |A|} -1 ) choose ... no.\\n              
                             # Instead, note that any two subsets A             
                             and B are disjoint iff their masks do              
                             not overlap. The total number of pairs             
                             of subsets (regardless of gcd) is (2^n             
                             -1)^2 - (2^n -1)  (ordered pairs minus             
                             those not disjoint).\\n        # But we            
                             need only those with gcd(A)=gcd(B)=v.              
                             The known working approach for small n             
                             is to iterate all pairs of masks M1,M2             
                             with M1 & M2 ==0, and count if                     
                             gcd(M1)==gcd(M2)==v. This is O(3^n) but            
                             for n=13 it\'s 1594323 pairs which is              
                             acceptable.\\n        # So here\'s the             
                             final implementation using this double             
                             loop over masks:\\n\\n        #                    
                             Precompute for each mask its gcd\\n                
                             mask_gcd = [0]*(1<<n)\\n        for                
                             mask in range(1, 1<<n):\\n            g            
                             = 0\\n            for i in range(n):\\n            
                             if mask & (1 << i):\\n                             
                             g = gcd(g, nums[i])\\n                             
                             mask_gcd[mask] = g\\n\\n        result             
                             = 0\\n        # Iterate over all pairs             
                             of masks A and B that are disjoint\\n              
                             for mask_a in range(1, 1<<n):\\n                   
                             sa = bin(mask_a).count(\'1\')  # size              
                             of A\\n            for mask_b in                   
                             range(mask_a+1, (1<<n):  # start from              
                             mask_a+1 to avoid double counting and              
                             self overlap\\n                if                  
                             (mask_a & mask_b) ==0:\\n                          
                             g_a = mask_gcd[mask_a]\\n                          
                             g_b = mask_gcd[mask_b]\\n                          
                             if g_a == g_b:\\n                                  
                             result = (result +                                 
                             all_gcd_counts.get(g_a,0)) % MOD  # No,            
                             wait all_gcd_counts is not used                    
                             here.\\n                        #                  
                             Actually, we should use the fact that              
                             all_gcd_counts is not needed if we                 
                             recompute gcds per mask each time (but             
                             it\'s already computed)\\n                         
                             # But in this approach, we need to                 
                             count for each pair (mask_a, mask_b) if            
                             gcd(mask_a)==gcd(mask_b)==v, then add              
                             all_gcd_counts[v] ? No, each pair is               
                             counted once.\\n                                   
                             # Alternatively, we should just check              
                             if gcd(mask_a)==gcd(mask_b), and then              
                             add 1 to the total if so.\\n                       
                             # Because the question asks for the                
                             number of ordered pairs (S,T) of                   
                             non-empty disjoint subsets with                    
                             gcd(S)=gcd(T). So each valid (mask_a,              
                             mask_b) with the conditions is one                 
                             pair.\\n                        # So               
                             the answer is the number of such mask              
                             pairs.\\n                        # But             
                             the sample input [1,2,3,4] n=4 has                 
                             answer 10.\\n                        #             
                             How many disjoint non-empty (A,B) pairs            
                             have gcd(A)=gcd(B)?\\n                             
                             # Manually for n=4, it\'s 10, so the               
                             double loop must return 10.\\n                     
                             # So the code should be:\\n                        
